e of back pain, skin changes, or constitutional symptoms (e.g., night sweats) argue against paraneoplastic brachial plexopathy as a primary cause?", "summary": "The evidence from multiple analyses suggests moderate support for brachial plexopathy (B1.1) and paraneoplastic brachial plexopathy from small cell lung carcinoma (B2.2), while diabetes-related ischemic mononeuropathy multiplex (B1.2) and apical lung tumor (B2.1) receive weak support. Vascular caus…"}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the absence of 2-3 toe syndactyly or abdominal obesity inconsistent with mononeuropathy multiplex as a metabolic/compressive cause?", "summary": "The evidence from multiple analyses suggests moderate support for brachial plexopathy (B1.1) and paraneoplastic brachial plexopathy from small cell lung carcinoma (B2.2), while diabetes-related ischemic mononeuropathy multiplex (B1.2) and apical lung tumor (B2.1) receive weak support. Vascular caus…"}, {"t": 2, "type": "SAFETY_ENSURE", "content": "Does the absence of specific paraneoplastic features (e.g., skin changes, organ-specific autoimmunity) rule out paraneoplastic neuropathy as a primary concern?", "summary": "The evidence from multiple analyses suggests moderate support for brachial plexopathy (B1.1) and paraneoplastic brachial plexopathy from small cell lung carcinoma (B2.2), while diabetes-related ischemic mononeuropathy multiplex (B1.2) and apical lung tumor (B2.1) receive weak support. Vascular caus…"}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the absence of chest symptoms (e.g., cough, dyspnea) inconsistent with apical lung tumor presenting with brachial plexopathy?", "summary": "The evidence from multiple analyses suggests moderate support for brachial plexopathy (B1.1) and paraneoplastic brachial plexopathy from small cell lung carcinoma (B2.2), while diabetes-related ischemic mononeuropathy multiplex (B1.2) and apical lung tumor (B2.1) receive weak support. Vascular caus…"}, {"t": 2, "type": "SAFETY_ENSURE", "content": "Does the absence of back pain, blurry vision, or skin changes rule out small cell lung carcinoma as the paraneoplastic source?", "summary": "The evidence from multiple analyses suggests moderate support for brachial plexopathy (B1.1) and paraneoplastic brachial plexopathy from small cell lung carcinoma (B2.2), while diabetes-related ischemic mononeuropathy multiplex (B1.2) and apical lung tumor (B2.1) receive weak support. Vascular caus…"}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of 2-3 toe syndactyly or abdominal obesity disfavor metastatic thoracic neoplasm with brachial plexus compression?", "summary": "The evidence from multiple analyses suggests moderate support for brachial plexopathy (B1.1) and paraneoplastic brachial plexopathy from small cell lung carcinoma (B2.2), while diabetes-related ischemic mononeuropathy multiplex (B1.2) and apical lung tumor (B2.1) receive weak support. Vascular caus…"}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.323], ["B2", 0.315], ["B4", 0.161]]}, {"top": [["B2", 0.244], ["B1", 0.223], ["B1.1", 0.178]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No universal instability markers (airway compromise, severe respiratory compromise, shock, major haemodynamic instability, severe altered mental status, or uncontrolled haemorrhage) are present. Symptoms are subacute with no acute neurological deterioration (e.g., no sudden worsening, no focal stroke signs), and vital signs remain stable (BP 177/108 mmHg, HR 90/min, SpO2 98%). Time-critical syndromes (e.g., stroke, septic shock) are already excluded by the vignette analysis.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading terminal branch posterior (0.178) < 0.75 and dangerous alternatives (B2.1: 0.0839, B1.1: 0.178) remain unresolved with danger ≥0.7. Further discriminators exist in reopened branches (B2.1, B2.2) and live musculoskeletal options (B4.1-3)."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.55, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.1783122190770009, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Apical lung tumor"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Brachial plexopathy"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Cerebral infarction"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Scalenus anticus syndrome"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Subclavian steal syndrome"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Abnormality of musculoskeletal physiology', 'Abnormality of the brachial nerve plexus', 'Abnormality of the musculature', 'brachial plexopathy', 'Thoracic platyspondyly'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.323}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "Brachial Plexopathy (Non-Traumatic, Paraneoplastic/Inflammatory)", "posterior": 0.178, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 1}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 1, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2.2", "B2.1", "B5"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=43%, source=both]\n\nBrachial Plexopathy (Non-Traumatic, Paraneoplastic/Inflammatory) vs Paraneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy (Smoking-Related) from Small Cell Lung Carcinoma:\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant inheritance, back pain, blurry vision, bone pain, changes in skin color\n\nBrachial Plexopathy (Non-Traumatic, Paraneoplastic/Inflammatory) vs Cervical Myofascial Pain Syndrome with Radicular Mimicry:\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n  Favours second: abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal rapid eye movement sleep, abnormality of masticatory muscle, abnormality of the tongue muscle, abnormality of thyroid physiology\n\nParaneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy (Smoking-Related) from Small Cell Lung Carcinoma vs Cervical Myofascial Pain Syndrome with Radicular Mimicry:\n  Favours first:  back pain, blurry vision, bone pain, changes in skin color, chest pain or discomfort\n  Favours second: abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal rapid eye movement sleep, abnormality of masticatory muscle, abnormality of the tongue muscle, abnormality of thyroid physiology\n\nRelated to Paraneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy (Smoking-Related) from Small Cell Lung Carcinoma: bronchopulmonary tumor, combined small cell lung carcinoma, lung carcinoma\n\nRelated to Cervical Myofascial Pain Syndrome with Radicular Mimicry: myopathy, temporomandibular joint dysfunction syndrome\n\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm' (score=0.79):]\n  Source: Management of Lung Cancer Invading the Superior Sulcus. > History and Physical\n  Pancoast syndrome refers to the collective group of symptoms associated with these tumors, which is secondary to the involvement of brachial plexus and associated chest wall, vertebral, or rib pain. S\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm' (score=0.79):]\n  Source: Management of Lung Cancer Invading the Superior Sulcus. > Pathophysiology\n  Pancoast or superior sulcus tumors, when they compress or invade the surrounding structures, cause a group of symptoms collectively called Pancoast syndrome. They typically involve the brachial plexus\n[RAG context for 'The patient states he has experienced these symptoms for a few weeks' (score=0.70):]\n  Source: Cancer treatment and survivorship statistics, 2016. > History and Physical\n  Neurologic paraneoplastic syndromes are immune-mediated syndromes associated with SCLC and include Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS), encephalomyelitis, limbic encephalitis, cerebellar ataxia, \n[RAG context for 'The patient states he has experienced these symptoms for a few weeks' (score=0.72):]\n  Source: Management of Lung Cancer Invading the Superior Sulcus. > History and Physical\n  Pancoast syndrome refers to the collective group of symptoms associated with these tumors, which is secondary to the involvement of brachial plexus and associated chest wall, vertebral, or rib pain. S\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  right arm weakness with progressive deterioration over weeks → steroid-induced muscle hypertrophy causing brachial plexus compression → Scalenus Anticus Syndrome (Hypertrophy-Induced Brachial Plexus Compression)\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for scalenus anticus muscle hypertrophy via MRI/CT of cervical spine and confirm brachial plexus compression\n  progressive arm weakness with weight loss and smoking history → lung cancer-related paraneoplastic syndrome → Paraneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy (Smoking-Related) from Small Cell Lung Carcinoma\n    (frequency: rare)\n    Suggestion: Order chest imaging (CT) to investigate for apical lung tumor or occult SCLC as potential paraneoplastic cause\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
